WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:06.080
Howdy folks. My throat is feeling better enough&nbsp;
to record a youtube video, so here I am. I plan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:09.920
to upload weekly for the next month to clear&nbsp;
out the backlog of videos I’ve been working on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:13.600
so hopefully that can fill the deep hole&nbsp;
in your heart that I’m all but certain my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:18.040
absence has wrought. I also have a big video&nbsp;
about manabases coming up later this month,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:22.520
and with that in mind I figure I may as well&nbsp;
run back through a subject that is near and dear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:29.040
to my heart—land counts. Now, I did talk about&nbsp;
this a bit in my lands video almost a year ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:32.560
but there was a bunch of other stuff in that video&nbsp;
and my thinking has developed a bit since then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:36.960
so I figure this video can serve as a more&nbsp;
targeted guide. The question I’ll be looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.840
to help you answer in this video is “how many&nbsp;
lands should my deck have in it,” and perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:46.680
more specifically, “should my deck have more&nbsp;
lands in it?” To help me out with answering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:51.440
this question I’ll be using a deck I built&nbsp;
in March of last year—my Radha battlecruiser&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:56.000
deck. You can watch the video about it if you&nbsp;
want, but the relevant information for this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.000
video is that it is a deck with a mana dork&nbsp;
commander, a pile of four mana ramp spells,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.520
and a bunch of 6 and 7 drops filling out the&nbsp;
rest of the deck, resulting in this deranged mana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:13.000
curve. It also runs 41 lands. A bunch of people&nbsp;
have made decks riffing off this deck, which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:18.120
super cool! I always love to see my decks being&nbsp;
an inspiration for people. What I’ve also noticed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:23.640
though, is that a common first change for people&nbsp;
is cutting 3-4 lands from the deck. I guess people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:27.680
must assume that I pulled the number 41 out of a&nbsp;
hat, or maybe that’s just my cute little personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:32.640
preference. I know I’ve frequently seen land&nbsp;
counts referenced online as an across-the-board&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:37.480
preference of sorts for people, like you’ll see&nbsp;
people say that they prefer 35 lands or 36 lands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:42.680
or that they’re a 38 land sort of person, and&nbsp;
other such statements. I suppose maybe people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:46.680
consider setting the land count of a deck they’re&nbsp;
adapting to their preferred number as a part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:51.920
putting their own spin on it. Maybe. I’ve also&nbsp;
seen people react with shock or skepticism when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:56.920
I mention that one of my decks has a land count&nbsp;
in the 40s, so maybe people just aren’t sold on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.920 --> 00:02:01.720
a count that high. Regardless, I want to discuss&nbsp;
the different factors that should influence land&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:06.640
count, discussing how each of them applied to&nbsp;
this deck as an example, and at the end of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:10.760
process maybe it’ll be a bit more clear why I went&nbsp;
with the number I went with, and you might have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:15.680
bit more insight on how many lands might be right&nbsp;
for your deck based on its particular attributes.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:19.760
For starters, let’s talk about mana curve—mana&nbsp;
curve refers to the mana costs of different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:24.720
cards in a deck, and on the most basic level the&nbsp;
average mana cost of a deck is an indicator of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:30.040
how much mana that deck needs to do what it wants&nbsp;
to do. As a general pattern, lower curved decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:35.240
will want less mana than higher curved decks, and&nbsp;
will thus also want a lower land count than higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:41.440
curved decks. However, in the context of commander&nbsp;
this is more of a correlation than a rule,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:46.040
and there are two main factors that contribute&nbsp;
to this correlation. One of these factors is how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:51.000
quickly a deck is looking to win the game, with&nbsp;
lower curved decks typically winning earlier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:56.480
than higher-curved decks. Power level and how&nbsp;
proactive a deck is will both affect how quickly a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:02.800
deck looks to be closing out games, but regardless&nbsp;
this land-count-vs-win-speed pattern tends to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:08.480
reasonably consistent one to see. Put simply, you&nbsp;
don’t need to play a land on turn 7 if there is no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:13.640
turn 7. Another way that mana curve can influence&nbsp;
the number of lands a deck wants is in terms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:18.600
how accessible the deck’s ways to dig for more&nbsp;
lands are. A deck with lots of low-cost draw and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:22.920
filtering can afford to run a lower land count&nbsp;
as long as it can find the colors to cast those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:28.040
digging cards, whereas decks that feature draw and&nbsp;
filtering that costs four or more mana will want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:32.520
to run a higher count in order to consistently&nbsp;
get enough mana to pay for that draw or filtering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:37.600
or at least supplement with ramp. Likewise,&nbsp;
decks whose source of dig is more complex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:42.480
such as a deck that needs both a Garruk’s&nbsp;
Uprising and some big creatures to trigger it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:45.760
will want to be careful to run enough&nbsp;
lands to avoid getting stalled out on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:49.680
mana before getting the chance to fulfill&nbsp;
the condition that will draw them into more.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:54.640
Most of my reasoning so far has been in terms&nbsp;
of how low a count a deck can afford to run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:59.560
but it’s also useful to think about the question&nbsp;
of mana curve in terms of how high a count a deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:05.760
can afford to run. More complex or mana-expensive&nbsp;
forms of draw also typically draw more cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:10.360
and if a deck has a lot of heavy duty draw—such&nbsp;
as a green deck running a number of cards that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:15.320
draw cards equal to a creature’s power—it can be&nbsp;
possible to run quite high land counts without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:19.880
really feeling negative impacts from it. What&nbsp;
happens if you draw 10 cards and then end the turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:25.000
with a hand containing six lands and 8 nonland&nbsp;
cards? You discard four or five lands, that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:30.320
what. When a deck can readily access powerful draw&nbsp;
that is a bit mana expensive, running a higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:35.320
land count is often an all-around win—either you&nbsp;
haven’t gotten enough mana to play the draw yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:39.600
in which case a high land count helps you have a&nbsp;
higher chance of getting there, or you have played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:43.720
the draw, in which case you’re churning through&nbsp;
enough cards to render that slightly increased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:49.800
land density negligible. What this relates to,&nbsp;
really, is how good a deck is at spending its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:54.760
mana. Let’s think about what we’re worried about&nbsp;
when adding more lands to a deck—it’s flooding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:59.280
right? We’re worried that we’ll draw a bunch of&nbsp;
lands, and instead of having cool, impactful plays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:04.040
to make, we’ll be stuck sitting around with a&nbsp;
hand full of lands. This is a valid concern,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:08.840
and *one* way to mitigate it is to run fewer&nbsp;
lands, but another way is to make sure your deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:14.080
is consistently able to churn through excesses&nbsp;
of mana. Having a higher curve alone will provide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:19.400
some insulation against getting flooded, since&nbsp;
playing a six drop is twice as card-efficient as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:24.360
playing two three-drops. This doesn’t necessarily&nbsp;
mean that you should pump up your curve to avoid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:29.160
getting flooded, but it does mean that drawing&nbsp;
a lot of lands will be more problematic for some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:34.760
decks than others. As far as tools reduce flooding&nbsp;
go, the sort of big draw I mentioned earlier is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:39.360
a fantastic way to improve a deck’s ability to&nbsp;
spend mana, and there are a few different options&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:44.800
here—decks can run a lot of it, decks can run less&nbsp;
of it and run filtering or tutors to find their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:49.920
way to it, and decks can also just put it in the&nbsp;
command zone. There are also other ways to improve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:55.600
ability to spend mana—MDFCs can be nice nonland&nbsp;
plays if a land isn’t needed, and there are also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:01.880
impactful mana-sinks like X costs and multi-use&nbsp;
activated abilities. Utility lands can be another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:06.560
great way to avoid running out of gas as long as&nbsp;
their abilities are relevant to a deck’s goals.

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:10.920
So, to pivot over to our example for this video,&nbsp;
how does my Radha deck do on these criteria that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:15.440
I’ve mentioned? In terms of mana-curve, it&nbsp;
has a horrendously high average nonland card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:22.320
cost of 5.33, and nothing below 4 mana in the&nbsp;
99. Its cheapest ways to dig for additional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:27.760
mana are some draw effects at 4 mana that require&nbsp;
additional game actions to actually begin drawing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:31.920
and I guess some cascade cards on five mana? You&nbsp;
could say that the deck’s ramp helps it out in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:37.560
terms of getting lands, but this deck would&nbsp;
much rather ramp and hit its land drops—each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:42.560
land is effectively worth two mana, meaning that&nbsp;
every land drop that the deck needs to ramp out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:48.080
rather than playing normally bears an expensive&nbsp;
opportunity cost. I bumped the land count from 40&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:54.000
up to 41 specifically because I had too many games&nbsp;
where I was having precisely this experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:59.960
effectively paying mana for land drops that I&nbsp;
could be making normally. Okay, so high-curved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:04.680
and a high-threshold to dig for more lands—these&nbsp;
features tell me that a low land count would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:08.560
tricky to pull off with this deck. But what&nbsp;
about its ability to spend mana in effective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:13.800
ways? For one, the high curve alone gives the&nbsp;
deck a nice boost in maintaining cards in hand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:18.160
It just can’t play cards very fast early in&nbsp;
the game, so its hand size stays big for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:22.400
while into the game even as it’s spending similar&nbsp;
amounts of mana per turn as the rest of the table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:26.680
and sometimes more. In addition, though the&nbsp;
number of draw spells in this deck isn’t wildly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:31.440
high—like 9 or 10 if I’m being generous about&nbsp;
what I consider draw—the deck is sees a ton of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:36.040
cards due to the amount of cascade in it, and&nbsp;
the fact that several of the draw spells cost 4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:40.480
mana means that they pop up reasonably often&nbsp;
due to the nature of the mechanic. Finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:44.880
I play a handful of mana sinks such as Captivating&nbsp;
Crew for the situations when the deck has an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:49.440
absurd amount of mana but is low on places to&nbsp;
put it, and I also have a Panglacial Wurm for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:54.160
when I topdeck a ramp spell and would quite like a&nbsp;
wurm to go along with it. With all of these tools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:59.560
for spending mana, I rarely feel flooded with&nbsp;
this deck even though the deck runs 41 lands.

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:02.960
So, that covers the biggest factors&nbsp;
that influence a deck’s land count,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:07.480
but let’s talk about a handful of others that can&nbsp;
also come into play in certain decks. For one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:12.240
the power of mulligans. You can watch my mulligan&nbsp;
video if you want a full rundown of this topic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:16.600
but the basic gist goes something like&nbsp;
this: depending on how they’re built,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:21.600
decks will use their mulligans to find some things&nbsp;
more than others. Some decks will be prioritizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:25.840
having a piece of ramp in their starting hand,&nbsp;
some will prioritize a synergy piece to go with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:30.880
their commander, some will prioritize a low-cost&nbsp;
creature, and some prioritize having 3 or more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:36.560
lands. In a probabilistic sense, this is kind&nbsp;
of a zero-sum activity—you can only use your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:41.360
mulligans to hunt for so many things, and if you&nbsp;
stretch yourself thin on this front you might find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:46.400
yourself dropping down to 5 card hands more often&nbsp;
than you’d like. What’s good to keep in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:51.200
then, is that running a land count closer to your&nbsp;
ideal land proportion in your opening hand gives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:56.080
you more wiggle room to use your mulligans&nbsp;
to hunt for nonland cards, and 42 is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.080 --> 00:09:01.920
number of lands in deck that offers the highest&nbsp;
chance for a 3 land hand. My Radha deck wants a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:07.120
piece of ramp every game without exception,&nbsp;
a fairly stringent goal for nonland cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:12.560
so running a land count close to this 3 land&nbsp;
proportion means more 3-4 land hands and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:17.920
ability to aggressively mulligan for ramp. Your&nbsp;
deck may be more flexible in what nonland cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:22.440
it wants in its starting hand, in which case a&nbsp;
lower land density and a focus on mulliganing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:26.440
for lands may be a more worthwhile plan,&nbsp;
but it’s good to consider the tradeoffs.

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:31.120
Another reason somebody might want to run&nbsp;
a higher land count is that it can allow a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:36.000
deck to have extra lands in hand more of&nbsp;
the time. An obvious application of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:41.080
is extra land play cards—these are often some&nbsp;
of the most efficient ramp spells out there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:44.560
but being able to take full advantage of&nbsp;
them requires running a land count high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:49.760
enough to have extra lands on hand. A less&nbsp;
obvious application is that a higher land&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:54.240
count gives a player greater control on the&nbsp;
timing of when certain lands are played. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:59.440
32 land deck will often need to play a Bojuka&nbsp;
Bog on curve in order to not miss a land drop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:03.800
and this might end up greatly reducing the&nbsp;
effectiveness of the card’s ETB. The same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:09.600
goes for Mystic Sanctuary, Mortuary Mire, and&nbsp;
Talon Gates of Madara—getting substantial utility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:14.760
out of these lands without missing a land&nbsp;
drop gets easier the more lands a deck runs.

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:19.960
Finally, let’s talk about one reason some decks&nbsp;
might want fewer lands. Some decks care about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.960 --> 00:10:24.560
what’s on top of their deck more than others,&nbsp;
an attribute that I like to call The Cantrip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:29.560
Coefficient. An example of a deck with a higher&nbsp;
Cantrip Coefficient would be a conventional build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:34.700
of a commander such as Jhoira, Weatherlight&nbsp;
Captain. Most Jhoira decks run lots of low&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:40.000
cost artifacts, often alongside cost-reducers,&nbsp;
and then seek to cast a ton of artifact spells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:45.080
in a single turn. This is a playstyle that&nbsp;
draws lots of cards one at a time and wants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.080 --> 00:10:50.640
drawn cards to be either an artifact or&nbsp;
an artifact payoff card, and as a result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:55.280
these decks will generally want to run as few&nbsp;
lands as they can get away with. Storm decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:59.520
as many Jhoira decks might be considered,&nbsp;
care more about the top of their library&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:05.200
than just about any other type of deck, and will&nbsp;
thus usually try to run a very low land count. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:10.080
lot of enchantress and spellslinger decks will&nbsp;
also rank highly on the cantrip coefficient,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:15.680
wanting to run as many of their respective card&nbsp;
types as possible. Even my Sharuum Slide deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:19.920
could fit into this mold to some extent with&nbsp;
its relationship with cycling cards, though the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:24.800
existence of cycling lands and landcycling cards&nbsp;
makes its relationship with lands quite different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:30.920
than these other decks. The biggest factor here&nbsp;
is that these are high-velocity decks, decks that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.920 --> 00:11:36.280
churn through cards at a fast rate, and also decks&nbsp;
that run lots of low cost cards. On the other end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.280 --> 00:11:40.880
of things you’ve got decks with larger-scale&nbsp;
draw, particularly ones that also have lots of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:46.320
high-cost cards. My Radha deck is more on this&nbsp;
end of things—it runs a couple cards that draw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:51.120
cards one at a time like Beast Whisperer and&nbsp;
Soul of the Harvest, but outside of topdeck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:55.840
situations with these in play it doesn’t care&nbsp;
too much about what percentage of lands it draws.

00:11:55.840 --> 00:12:00.640
So, that’s how I came to the land count in this&nbsp;
particular deck. Very high average mana cost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:05.680
high threshold to access draw, strong ability to&nbsp;
spend mana and maintain cards in hand, and a need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:10.480
to mulligan aggressively for ramp rather than for&nbsp;
lands. You can follow a process for this, like I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:14.320
did in this video, and I’m showing a checklist on&nbsp;
screen right now that you can screenshot if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:19.760
like, though this is a rather crude summary that&nbsp;
shouldn’t be treated as gospel for every deck. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:25.360
should also note that though I position 35-37 as&nbsp;
a moderate count, with the number of MDFCs and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.360 --> 00:12:29.800
utility lands we have these days I’d guess that&nbsp;
the median casual EDH deck would actually be best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.800 --> 00:12:34.960
served by a land count of somewhere around 38.&nbsp;
You can even use a formula like that suggested by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.960 --> 00:12:40.360
Frank Karsten if you really want to be handed&nbsp;
an exact number. However, both that and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:46.280
guidelines I’ve suggested can only give an initial&nbsp;
estimate, and after that it’s a matter of testing.

00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:50.840
When playing a deck, try to keep track: how&nbsp;
often are you finding yourself wishing you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:55.560
had a land to play, and how often are you finding&nbsp;
yourself wishing you had a nonland card to play?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.560 --> 00:13:00.720
Importantly, pay attention to lesser cases&nbsp;
of these, not just the mega-floods and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:05.320
mega-screws—I’ve seen players consistently&nbsp;
miss land drops over the course of several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.320 --> 00:13:11.800
games before hitting a 1 in 20 game flood and&nbsp;
taking it as a cue to cut lands. If both land&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.800 --> 00:13:16.960
shortages and nonland shortages are happening, is&nbsp;
it possible to inject some additional ways to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:20.680
cards in hand into the deck rather than cutting&nbsp;
lands? Cut out deadweight cards that aren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.680 --> 00:13:24.720
improving your deck’s ability to maintain a fluid&nbsp;
gameplan to give you more space to work with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:30.040
or replace some combination of ramp, draw, and&nbsp;
removal with versions that are more concentrated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.040 --> 00:13:35.120
if you can afford to without making your deck too&nbsp;
clunky. Adding an MDFC or a utility land can also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:39.440
be a great way to improve the feel of your deck’s&nbsp;
card/mana balance, to allow the deck to hit the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:44.560
same number of land drops but have more ways to&nbsp;
spend mana, or give an additional land drop while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:49.480
maintaining that slot as a way to spend mana,&nbsp;
depending on what’s being cut. Modern Horizons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:56.420
III had both a lot of great MDFCs and a cycle of&nbsp;
pretty lovely utility lands, and in the post-MH3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.420 --> 00:14:02.200
EDH landscape I truly don’t understand people&nbsp;
who are clinging onto low-mid-30s land counts for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.200 --> 00:14:07.800
their conventionally-curved midrange EDH decks.&nbsp;
Add lands! They let you cast spells! There’s also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:13.200
the question of how to navigate colors in your&nbsp;
lands, but that’s a question for another day.

00:14:13.200 --> 00:14:15.360
Thanks for watching! And thanks&nbsp;
to Archidekt for supporting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:19.320
channel. Remember how I mentioned how land&nbsp;
counts affect mulligans? With Archidekt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.320 --> 00:14:23.560
there’s a hypergeometric calculator a short scroll&nbsp;
away that can give you an immediate sense of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:27.440
likely you are to draw various numbers of&nbsp;
different card types. Pretty dang cool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:32.520
if you ask me. I’d also like to thank my lovely&nbsp;
patrons, who are on-screen now. I just opened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.520 --> 00:14:36.800
submissions for my deck review for this month, so&nbsp;
if you want a chance to get your deck reviewed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:41.640
me, you can sign up for my patreon today&nbsp;
for three bucks a month! Alright, bye.

